<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/advanced/property-law">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    "Ownership" sounds like a single fact; property law shows it is a bundle of rights — use,
    exclude, transfer, mortgage, bequeath — sliced through time and shared with neighbours,
    lenders, tenants and the state. The chapter's two halves prove the same point from different
    directions: land law decomposes <em>tangible</em> ownership into estates and interests held
    simultaneously by many hands, and intellectual property engineers <em>intangible</em>
    monopolies with deliberately limited terms. Both are answers to one question: what shape
    should exclusive control take so the asset is used well?
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the bundle of rights, from land to intellectual property" widget="equationStep" config={propCfg} />

  <h2>Tenure and estates: nobody owns land outright</h2>
  <p>
    English land law's deep structure is feudal in origin: the Crown owns all land; everyone else
    holds an <strong>estate</strong> — a slice of time. The <strong>fee simple absolute in
    possession</strong> ("freehold") is the nearest thing to ownership: indefinite, freely
    transferable, bequeathable. The <strong>leasehold</strong> is a term of years carved out of
    it — the landlord's reversion and the tenant's term coexist in the same land, which is why a
    lease is itself an estate that can be sold, mortgaged and underlet. Life estates and the
    doctrines of future interests (remainders, reversions — rights that ripen later) complete the
    time-slicing; the modern user is the flat on a 125-year lease, whose "ownership" is a
    depreciating term in someone else's freehold — a structure most buyers discover only when the
    term's length starts moving their mortgage offers. The <strong>lease versus licence</strong>
    distinction carries real weight: a lease confers an estate (protection, security of tenure,
    assignable), a licence only a permission (revocable, no estate) — and the courts look through
    labels to substance (<em>Street v Mountford</em>: "exclusive possession at a rent for a term"
    makes a lease whatever the paperwork calls it — the anti-evasion rule that protects
    occupants).
  </p>
  <p>
    Around estates orbit <strong>interests</strong> held by third parties. <strong>Easements</strong>
    (rights of way, drainage, light through defined windows) burden one parcel for another's
    benefit and run with the land — binding successors whatever hands change. <strong>Freehold
    covenants</strong> (promises touching land — maintain a fence, pay estate charges) bind
    successors in title through doctrine (and, for flats, statute — the leasehold-enforcement
    machinery). <strong>Mortgages</strong> are the financial layer: the lender's security
    interest making the modern property market possible — most "homeowners" co-own their title
    with a bank for decades. Registration is the administrative revolution that makes all this
    governable: the Land Registration Act 2002's register-as-everything logic (and its Torrens-system
    cousins across the Commonwealth) replaced the chain-of-deeds archaeology with a state-guaranteed
    register — title by database, with the register's mirror, curtain and insurance principles
    defining what buyers can rely on. Compulsory purchase completes the public overlay: the state
    may take land for infrastructure, against compensation at market value (plus disturbance) —
    expropriation governed by law, the universal test of a functioning property system.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Adverse possession</strong> is the doctrine that most startles outsiders: possess
    another's land openly, exclusively, without permission, for the limitation period (12 years
    for unregistered land) and the true owner's claim is barred — title shifting to the
    possessor. The logic: diligence over dormancy, and the barring of stale claims. Registered
    land under the 2002 Act defanged the surprise (the registered owner gets notice, two years
    to object) — a century's quiet policy dispute (certainty of registered title versus the
    sleeping-owner penalty) settled by procedure.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "property is absolute — my land, my rules." Every ownership is
    already relative: planning law constrains use, utilities hold statutory access rights,
    neighbours hold easements and nuisance claims (the tort lesson's unreasonable-interference
    standard), the state holds compulsory-purchase and taxing powers, and (in England) the
    deepest layer — the freeholder's reversion under your lease — is not yours at all. The
    American framing ("property is a bundle of sticks, and regulation takes sticks") is the same
    insight: ownership is a legal allocation, continuously renegotiated, not a natural absolute.
  </p>

  <h2>Intellectual property: monopolies by design</h2>
  <p>
    IP solves a specific economic problem: intangibles are <em>non-rival</em> (my copying your
    song costs you nothing) and <strong>public-good-like</strong> (costly to create, costless to
    copy) — so without protection, creation is under-supplied; with perfect protection, culture
    and follow-on innovation starve. Each IP right is a different settlement of that trade-off.
    <strong>Patents</strong> (~20 years from filing): the disclosure bargain — full technical
    teaching published to the world in exchange for a temporary monopoly on the invention, tested
    by novelty, inventive step and industrial applicability; patents on obvious or already-known
    subject-matter fail (the pharmaceutical "evergreening" disputes are the modern frontier of
    the inventive-step standard). <strong>Copyright</strong> protects particular
    <em>expression</em> — never ideas, facts or methods (the idea-expression dichotomy is the
    system's load-bearing wall) — for the author's life plus 70 years, with fair-dealing and
    fair-use exceptions keeping the commons usable; term extensions (the US lobby-driven
    20-year extensions of the 1990s) keep the public-domain question politically alive.
    <strong>Trademarks</strong> protect signs identifying commercial origin — renewable
    indefinitely so long as used, because brand goodwill is a continuing investment; <strong>trade
    secrets</strong> protect information as long as it stays secret (the Coca-Cola formula, the
    industrial default where patents' disclosure price is too high). The durations are not
    arbitrary: the more the right resembles monopoly over something society needs to build on
    (inventions), the shorter and more conditional; the more it protects against deception
    (brands), the more renewable.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Worked example of the bargain's arithmetic:</strong> a drug costs £800m to develop
    and £20m to copy. Without a patent, imitators price at marginal cost within a year and
    nothing recovers the £800m. With a 20-year patent, the innovator prices at monopoly levels
    long enough to recover development plus return — and at expiry, the formula is free forever,
    with the patent document itself a complete public recipe. The system's cost (a generation of
    monopoly pricing) is the deliberate price of the disclosure and the incentive; generic
    competition on patent expiry is the designed payoff. Every IP policy argument — term lengths,
    compulsory licensing for public health (the TRIPS flexibilities from the law-international
    lesson), orphan works, AI-generated works — is a renegotiation of this arithmetic's terms.
  </p>

  <h2>Leases in depth: the estate and its protections</h2>
  <p>
    The lease deserves its own treatment because it is where property and contract interlock.
    Essentials (<em>Street v Mountford</em>): exclusive possession, for a term, at a rent —
    with the paper's label immaterial (sham "licences" are leases if the substance matches;
    the exceptions are genuinely service-based occupancy and the institutional categories).
    The estate structure generates its characteristic problems: <strong>covenants run both
    ways</strong> — the tenant's covenants (repair, use) bind assignees of the burden through
    the benefit-and-burden rule on assignment, the landlord's covenants (quiet enjoyment,
    non-derogation from grant) bind the reversion; <strong>security of tenure</strong> statutes
    (the residential assured-tenancy and long-leasehold regimes; business tenancies' statutory
    renewal right) overlay the contract with protection — the political economy of housing
    law in statutory form. The contemporary disputes are structural: England's leasehold
    houses (buying a house yet paying ground rent on someone else's freehold — the reform
    programme's target), building-safety costs migrating to leaseholders, and the commonhold
    alternative (unit-ownership of flats with a communal freehold — standard worldwide, rare
    in England, now legislated for revival). A lease is the case study showing property law
    is the allocation of risk across time and persons, not a natural absolute.
  </p>

  <h2>Registration worked: a purchase and what the register does</h2>
  <p>
    Follow a registered-land purchase. The buyer's solicitor obtains official copies: the
    property register (title class — absolute, qualified, possessory; absolute title is the
    state's guarantee to the stated extent), the proprietorship register, and the charges
    register (mortgages, easements, covenants). The three principles do the work:
    <strong>mirror</strong> (the register reflects title), <strong>curtain</strong> (equitable
    interests behind trusts stay off the register — the buyer need not search behind it, with
    <strong>overriding interests</strong> the documented exceptions: actual occupation, short
    leases, easements by long use), and <strong>insurance</strong> (the state indemnifies
    losses from register errors). <strong>Worked example:</strong> the buyer inspects, notices
    an elderly occupant, proceeds without inquiry. After completion, the occupant claims an
    overriding interest by actual occupation — and may bind the buyer (the <em>Chhokar</em>-
    line cases: occupation visible or reasonably discoverable can override registration). The
    2002 Act's response was to shrink overriding interests — most now require protection on
    the register at first registration of the owner's title. The policy direction is
    explicit: push interests onto the register so the mirror reflects and the curtain thins.
    Registration is not clerical work; it is the state's answer to the fundamental question
    of who can safely rely on what.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The numerus clausus principle.</strong> Property systems closed the list of
    proprietorship types: you may not invent a novel property right (an estate "on condition the
    buyer never sells to X religion" is unenforceable) — because freely customisable property
    rights would tax every third party dealing with the asset; contract binds two parties,
    property binds the world, so the world gets a vote on property's forms. The principle
    explains why new assets (domain names, Bitcoin, game items, data) strain for recognition:
    they must fit, or the legislature must extend, the closed list. <strong>Data is the live
    case:</strong> personal data is economically property-like, morally contested (persons are
    not owners of themselves in the way owners own things), and currently governed by
    <em>rights of control</em> (GDPR's access, erasure, portability) rather than ownership — a
    deliberate doctrinal choice now under pressure from platform economics.
    <strong>IP's second-century problems.</strong> Patent thickets and hold-ups in
    standard-essential patents (the FRAND licensing battles between handset makers); copyright's
    orphan works and the automated-content-matching systems adjudicating expression at platform
    scale; AI training on copyrighted corpora (the pending litigation over whether training is
    infringement or fair dealing is the era's defining IP question — its answer reallocates
    trillions in value between creators, platforms and models); and genome editing's patent
    contests (the CRISPR fights are patent doctrine in action).
    <strong>Open questions:</strong> does the leasehold/communal-ownership balance survive the
    affordability crisis (England's leasehold-reform programme is the live experiment); can
    property thinking coherently extend to the metaverse's ephemeral assets; and whether
    digital-exhaustion and resale rights for digital goods (your "bought" ebook is licensed, not
    owned — the first-sale doctrine's digital gap) will close, since every consumer-facing
    industry now structures around the gap.
  </p>
  <p>
    Environmental and climate dimensions complete the modern picture. Property's rigidity
    meets a changing baseline: coastal boundaries (accretion and erosion doctrines — the
    movable freehold problem as seas rise), water rights built for abundance under drought,
    and land-use law as the primary climate instrument (zoning's supply effects, floodplain
    buyouts, conservation easements — permanent encumbrances negotiated through the property
    system's own tools). Compulsory purchase and the compensation principle face their
    hardest cases where regulation devalues without taking title — the regulatory-takings
    debates, sharpest in US doctrine, resolved in the UK through the planning system's
    compensation architecture. The scholarly synthesis: property law is society's settlement
    with the future — duration, succession and irrevocability are its core promises — and
    climate change is testing every one of them at once. The land law of the twenty-first
    century is being written as adaptation doctrine, and this chapter's readers are looking
    at its first drafts.
  </p>

